# Level 2 → 3 — Reading a File with Spaces in Its Name

## Level Goal
Read the password stored in a file called `spaces in this filename`.

## Description
Shells use spaces as argument separators. Without proper handling, each word in the filename is interpreted as a separate argument. This level teaches quoting and escaping techniques.

## Approach
After logging in as `bandit2`, running the command without quotes predictably failed. The correct approach was to quote the entire filename:

```bash
cat "./--spaces in this filename--"
```

Alternatively, escape each space with a backslash:

```bash
cat spaces\ in\ this\ filename
```

> Password obtained. [Not shared per OverTheWire rules]

## Key Takeaway
Wrapping a filename in double quotes forces the shell to treat the entire string as a single argument. Tab completion handles escaping automatically and is the fastest approach in practice.
